  "ai_language_tip": "Tetum (tet) is the street language but Bageo cannot speak it; try Portuguese (pt) with offices and older staff, Indonesian (id) with many shopkeepers, and English with NGOs, hotels and expat-facing cafés in Dili.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Common in Dili hotels, NGO-linked businesses and some clinics; rare in markets, kiosks and rural shops.",
  "politeness": "Warm and personal; greet with bondia/botarde, use Senhor or Senhora, and allow a little small talk before the request.",
  "name_tip": "Names mix Portuguese given names with family names; many people go by a nickname; spell names out because Portuguese spelling is not always used.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobiles have 8 digits starting with 7 (7723 4567); landlines keep 7 digits; there is no trunk zero.",
  "time_tip": "Times are said in Tetum or Portuguese with 24-hour in writing; 'tuku hitu' style hours are common, so repeat the number in digits.",
  "number_tip": "The currency is the US dollar, with local centavo coins; prices are quoted in dollars everywhere.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid the midday break around 12:00–14:00 and Sunday mornings, when many attend Mass; Catholic holidays close most offices.",
  "closed_days": "Offices close Saturday and Sunday; small shops open daily but may shut Sunday morning; Christmas, Easter and national days close government services.",
  "booking_channels": "Facebook pages and WhatsApp handle most bookings; calls still matter for small businesses; there is no big ride-hail app, so drivers are called directly; payment is mostly cash in dollars.",
  "voice_menu": "Telcos (Timor Telecom, Telkomcel, Telemor) and banks run short IVR menus; most small businesses answer the phone themselves.",
  "callback_habits": "Owners reply on WhatsApp or Facebook Messenger, sometimes hours later; drivers and guesthouses call back from a different mobile.",
